    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d331">

                <pb facs="0412" n="398" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg398"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.105/97</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2494</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-26">April 26,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received June 3.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose a copy of
                    despatch no. 101 of March 28, 1944,<anchor n="20" xml:id="d331-f1"/> from the
                    Secretary on detail at Sian in regard to the activities of General Tai Li, head
                    of the Generalissimo’s secret police.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Summary</hi>. General Tai has been traveling through
                        Shensi and Honan Provinces recently in connection with the functioning of
                        his <pb facs="0413" n="399" xml:id="pg399"/> intelligence organizations and
                        the creation of a Kuomintang organization which will work for the
                        reassertion of the Central Government’s authority in north China, which has
                        been progressively lost to the Chinese Communists. General Tai is reliably
                        quoted as stating that the Communist question is of more importance than
                        that of Japan in so far as China is concerned. General Tai is said to feel
                        that it is important to win over the puppet officials to the Central
                        Government and that one means of doing so is to give them guarantees that
                        they are not to be molested after the conclusion of the war. Most puppet
                        officials apparently desire such an arrangement as they are convinced Japan
                        cannot win the war. General Tai is described by an American observer<anchor
                            n="21" xml:id="d331-f2"/> as having as great power and influence as ever
                        and as being increasingly entrusted by the Generalissimo with carrying out
                        various tasks. <hi rendition="#italic">End of Summary.</hi>
                    </p>
                </quote>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
